Hi Ruben,

Unfortunately, this is a tricky problem that cannot easily be solved by oSCR.  You might need the integration of an explicit movement model (e.g., Gardner et al. 2022 <https://doi.org/10.1002/ecy.3771>) to accommodate the features in your data.  A good example is individual "ET2" in session 2, where they mostly use the northern portion of the study area and have reasonably clustered locations (just like ET1 and ET3), until they seem to venture south.  The locations do not need to perfectly fit a bivariate normal, but that southern movement by ET2 almost seems exploratory and would certainly violate the assumption.  It can cause the optimizer to get stuck when the starting values make the likelihood calculation fail.  This sensitivity to starting values has been an enduring problem for both oSCR and secr with regards to telemetry integration.

If individual movements varied by season, you could design your analysis such that it represents some portion of time during which movement can be easily described.  That is often the case with migratory species, where telemetry tracks might indicate migratory behavior that is outside the scope of the analysis.  But for mammals with complex behaviors, the ability to filter the data can become difficult to impossible.  Sometimes animals exhibit behavior that does not follow the assumptions of a simple model.

Note, there is an important difference between filtering your data based on some a priori justification that is reproducible and filtering by inspecting the data and culling "outliers" that are poorly defined.  I certainly would not recommend the latter.

